records.source_hypotheses:
Core: X is any topological space. CapHyp f C M z b s requires:
- (C1) M ∈ C and f ≤ b on C;
- (C2) f ≤ s on frontier C;
- (C3) b < f z, with a path from M to z along which f ≥ s.

saddle_cut adds (C2+), that every frontier point other than S is below s, and M ≠ S. elder_death_level_peak adds [CompactSpace X], [LocallyConnectedSpace X], Continuous f and f M = b. toTorus needs a homeomorphism φ : Y ≃ₜ (Fin d → ℝ) and a compact C.

ridge_capHyp takes one-variable ridge data:
- continuity of h on [-r/2, 2r];
- g' = F on [-2r, 2r];
- F(∓r/2) = 0;
- convexity of F - (x + r/2)(x - r/2)/8;
- the transverse maximum;
- the curved-side bound;
- ‖h(-r/2)‖ ≤ 2r;
- a gap below 9r³/32.

ridge_capHyp_E takes:
- FiniteDimensional E;
- f ∈ C⁴ on an open U ⊇ D = [-2r, 2r] × B̄(0, 2r);
- the block bound m on D³f, and |f_xxx| ≤ m on the pin segment;
- the fourth-order blocks ≤ n, with (H2) rn ≤ 1/20;
- D²f(M) ≤ -λ on transverse directions, with (H1) 8rm² < λ;
- critical pins;
- gap r³/6.

ridge_capHyp_blocks takes CAP (1) with partial-block norms M3 and M4, k > 0, (4/(3k)) r M3² < λ, r M4 ≤ 3k/10 and gap k r³.

The torus versions apply these to the lift g = f ∘ torusCover L d ∘ φ for a frame φ : ℝ × E ≃ₜ (Fin d → ℝ). The death-level version adds Fact (0 < L) and Continuous f.

